boundary until GATE-0006. | open |\n--\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-1-# Runbook-authoring allocation ledger\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-2-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-3-This readable companion to [`runbook-authoring-manifest.json`](runbook-authoring-manifest.json) is the persistent allocation ledger for the mock-fantasy-quest-4 authoring system. The JSON is authoritative for IDs, provenance, dependencies, output paths, counters, and recovery state. Accepted IDs are immutable: resume from the first non-accepted batch and append only at `B0007` and `R0013`.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-4-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-5-## Wave 1 \u2014 foundation contracts\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-6-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-7-| Order | Batch | R contracts (maximum 7) | Dependency / parallel disposition | Checkpoint |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-8-| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md:9:| 1 | B0001 | R0001 | GATE-0001; no parallel work | Record gate and R0001 ownership |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-10-| 2 | B0002 | R0002, R0003, R0010 | After B0001 | Validate seams, fixtures, tokens and retained gates |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-11-| 3 | B0003 | R0004, R0008 | After B0002 | Validate state slice and P0 service exclusion |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-12-| 4 | B0004 | R0005, R0006 | After B0003; R0006 waits for GATE-0003 | Validate content/save separation |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-13-| 5 | B0005 | R0007, R0009, R0011 | After B0004; R0007/GATE-0003 and R0009/GATE-0005 remain blocked | Validate migration, diagnostics, provenance |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-14-| 6 | B0006 | R0012 | After B0003 and B0004; safe in parallel with B0005 after B0004 | Cross-check foundation documentation and gates |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-15-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-16-The batches are deliberately smaller than seven where a contract is context-heavy (state, persistence, content) or tightly coupled to its predecessor. Together they form an incremental foundation vertical slice: reproducible baseline, shared seams/test harness, deterministic game state and P0 exclusion, content and recovery boundaries, then provenance/diagnostic controls and documented closure. They do not imply that the game is implemented.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-17-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-18-## Contract allocation and asset assessment\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-19-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-20-| R ID | IMP source | Writer | Output | Asset assessment |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-21-| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md:22:| R0001 | IMP-0001 | B0001 | `runbooks/R0001.md` | not applicable \u2014 tooling boundary only |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-23-| R0002 | IMP-0002 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0002.md` | not applicable \u2014 interfaces only |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-24-| R0003 | IMP-0003 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0003.md` | not applicable \u2014 deterministic fixtures only |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-25-| R0004 | IMP-0004 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0004.md` | not applicable \u2014 state envelopes only |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-26-| R0005 | IMP-0005 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0005.md` | not applicable \u2014 schema reserves later asset fields |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-27-| R0006 | IMP-0006 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0006.md` | not applicable \u2014 save/recovery protocol |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-28-| R0007 | IMP-0007 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0007.md` | not applicable \u2014 migrations only |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-29-| R0008 | IMP-0008 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0008.md` | not applicable \u2014 negative service scope |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-30-| R0009 | IMP-0009 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0009.md` | not applicable \u2014 local diagnostics only |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-31-| R0010 | IMP-0010 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0010.md` | not applicable \u2014 no final art/fonts/icons |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md-32-| R0011 | IMP-0011 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0011.md` | not applicable \u2014 provenance control only |\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"find projects -path '*/runbooks/R*.md' -maxdepth 5 -print | head -30; rg -n -C 5 '## (Metadata|Provenance|Asset|Scope|Implementation|Validation|Gate|Acceptance)' projects -g 'R*.md' | head -300; sed -n '1,180p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/PROJECT_BRIEF.md; sed -n '1,180p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/README.md; sed -n '1,160p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n# Product brief \u2014 Mock Fantasy Quest 4\n\n**Source of truth:** [`INITIAL.md`](INITIAL.md). This normalized brief supports\ndownstream planning; it preserves source scope and records unknowns rather than\nchoosing them.\n\n## Product problem and outcome\n\nCasual adult players need a compact, approachable fantasy RPG that delivers a\ncomplete story without requiring a long commitment or dependable connection.\nThe first useful session is successful when a new player finishes a first\nturn-based battle and understands how to save and resume. The release outcome\nis an original short campaign that a player can complete through exploration,\ndialogue, quests, combat, rewards, saving, and a short ending.\n\n## Category, users, and core experience\n\n| Area | Normalized first-release statement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Category | Deliberately small, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG. |\n| Users | Casual and adult players, including people using keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad, captions, adjustable text, and visual settings. |\n| Campaign boundary | One town, one dungeon, and one guided, short ending; no further regions or chapters are required. |\n| Core loop | Explore, converse, accept or advance a quest, enter party-based turn-based combat, manage rewards/inventory, save, and continue. |\n| Experience direction | Original warm hand-painted fantasy, readable silhouettes, and a restrained palette. |\n| Functional reference | *Final Fantasy V* may inform only the high-level scope of a party-based turn-based adventure. |\n\n## Selected target systems\n\n| Target | First-release role | Input model | Delivery constraints | Out-of-scope boundary |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Responsive public web application | Primary browser surface for the complete game. | Keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad. | Responsive phone/tablet/desktop layout; browser compatibility and performance budget must be approved before implementation. | Native binaries, a separate web-only campaign, and browser-exclusive mechanics. |\n| Progressive web app (PWA) | Installable delivery of the same shared game core. | Same inputs where the browser/device supports them. | Offline assets and save-data behavior; safe update/cache refresh must preserve confirmed player data. | A PWA-exclusive campaign, mandatory continuous connection, or a different ruleset. |\n\nThe product uses a shared core with platform adaptation only where necessary.\nIt should work on widely used devices and under feasible slow-network\nconditions; exact supported browsers, device classes, storage quotas, and\nnetwork floor are open decisions.\n\n## First-release scope and measurable success measures\n\nIncluded scope: playable exploration; one town and one dungeon; narrative,\ndialogue, quests, progression and a short ending; onboarding/tutorial; at least\none tutorialized turn-based battle; inventory and rewards; save/load, resume,\ncheckpoints, data-loss recovery; settings and accessibility; audio/visual\nfeedback; content/level delivery; and crash reporting with player-facing error\nrecovery. The source also names account/identity, multiplayer/player safety,\nlive-service operations, and purchase/entitlement handling, but their release\nstatus remains contingent on the explicit conflict decision below.\n\nThe release is measurable when:\n\n- A new player can finish the tutorial battle, create a save, reload it, and\n  reach the short ending on each supported target.\n- The delivered campaign contains exactly one town, one dungeon, and one short\n  ending; added regions or chapters are not needed for release completion.\n- Both targets meet their stated input role; the PWA is installable and each\n  target has a tested offline, save, update, and error-recovery path.\n- Dialogue, inventory, quest, and progression UI are readable with approved\n  accessibility settings, and captions/visual settings are available where the\n  corresponding feedback exists.\n- An interruption, recoverable error, or safe update offers a player-facing\n  recovery path and does not silently discard the last confirmed save.\n\n## Later roadmap, non-goals, and reference boundary\n\nDeferred roadmap: live events/seasonal content, player-created/shared content\n(levels, mods, designs, stories), advanced analytics, and advanced\nmonetisation. They are excluded from first-release acceptance.\n\nNon-goals: copied branding, assets, text, layouts, characters, story/lore,\naudio, maps, or distinctive interactions; additional towns/dungeons/campaigns;\nnative apps; and platform-specific game variants. No cloud-sync, payment,\nmoderation, service-uptime, or backend commitment is implied before approval.\n\n| Reference boundary | Prohibited | Permitted |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Branding and copy | Names, logos, trademarks, marketing copy, story text, dialogue, characters, and world/lore. | A high-level description of a compact party-based turn-based adventure. |\n| Assets and presentation | Art, music, sound, maps, screen composition, visual language, and layouts. | Original art and UI that fulfil this brief\u2019s own direction. |\n| Interaction design | Reproducing distinctive combat screens, menus, flows, quests, or interaction choreography. | Independently designed mechanics and feedback appropriate to the requested scope. |\n\n## Constraints and safe assumptions\n\nExplicit constraints: web/PWA delivery, offline play, accessibility,\nlocalisation, privacy, safe save/recovery, slow-network feasibility, and\noriginal expression. Safe working assumptions: the offline core does not block\non a remote service; content/UI are original; local persistence is durable\nenough for confirmed saves where browser storage permits; connected features,\nif approved, degrade visibly; and crash diagnostics minimize personal data.\nThese are guardrails, not approval of a stack, vendor, backend, account model,\ndata-retention policy, or technical promise.\n\n## Open decisions requiring human approval\n\n1. **Offline versus connected scope:** Reconcile the offline single-player\n   premise with required account identity, multiplayer safety, live operations,\n   and purchase/entitlement handling. Classify each as release-critical,\n   optional connected enhancement, or deferred.\n2. **Account and data policy:** Decide whether accounts are absent, optional,\n   or mandatory; then define authentication, sync/conflict behavior, retention,\n   deletion, and offline/service-outage recovery.\n3. **Multiplayer, commerce, and safety:** State whether multiplayer or\n   real-money purchases actually ship, plus age rating, regions, moderation,\n   reporting, refunds, store, and applicable legal obligations.\n4. **Technical delivery baseline:** Choose engine/framework, repository and CI\n   constraints, browser/device matrix, performance budget, offline-storage\n   quota, update activation policy, error-reporting provider, and cost model.\n5. **Production content and access:** Approve party size, encounter/quest\n   counts, combat/progression balance, language set, audio scope, and the exact\n   accessibility options before production planning.\n# Canonical specification index\n\nThis directory is the canonical, resumable specification for **mock-fantasy-quest-4**. It consolidates F001\u2013F010 without overriding [`INITIAL.md`](../INITIAL.md), which remains the source of truth. A later author must cite stable IDs here rather than rediscovering scope.\n\n## Control rules\n\n- **P0** is an original, offline-capable 2D single-player fantasy RPG: one town, one dungeon, a short guided campaign and ending, exploration, dialogue/quests, party turn combat, inventory, local saves/recovery, settings/accessibility, responsive web, and PWA distribution.\n- Target-neutral rules live in `requirements.*`; the web/PWA differences live in `platform-appendix.md`. PWA is a delivery adaptation of the shared game, not a second game.\n- `Final Fantasy V` is a functional scope reference for party-based turn adventure only. It permits no copied expression: names, lore, text, art, music, maps, layouts, UI flows, or interaction choreography.\n- `proposed` requirements and every `GATE-*` are not implementation authorisation. Resolve their linked `DEC-*` record before allocating dependent work.\n\n## Chapter register\n\n| Chapter ID | Document / owner | Depends on | State |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| SPEC-00 | `00-domain-discovery.md` / product discovery | INITIAL | complete |\n| SPEC-01 | `01-product-brief.md` / product | SPEC-00 | complete |\n| SPEC-02 | `02-feature-model.md` / game design | SPEC-00\u201301 | complete |\n| SPEC-03 | `03-technical-contract.md` / technical design | SPEC-00\u201302 | complete |\n| SPEC-04 | `04-experience-contract.md` / experience design | SPEC-00\u201303 | complete |\n| SPEC-11 | `requirements.md`, `requirements.json` / F011 specification owner | SPEC-00\u201304, planning 05\u201310 | complete |\n| SPEC-12 | `traceability-matrix.md` / F011 specification owner | SPEC-11 | complete; runbook IDs pending F012+ |\n| SPEC-13 | `decision-log.md` / product owner | SPEC-00\u201304 | open decisions recorded |\n| SPEC-14 | `platform-appendix.md` / technical + experience owner | SPEC-11 | complete; thresholds gated |\n| SPEC-15 | `domain-completeness-review.md` / F011 specification owner | SPEC-00\u201304, SPEC-11 | complete; gated domains retained |\n\n## Bounded expansion protocol\n\nExpand one domain chapter at a time, preserving IDs and this register: identify entities/rules, authoring inputs, dependencies, P0 boundary, target adaptations, verification, and open decisions. Proposed detailed chapters are shell/session, campaign content, combat, progression/inventory, persistence/recovery, experience/accessibility, PWA/release, and connected-scope holds. A chapter can move from `planned` to `complete` only when its requirements, trace rows, and decision/risk links exist; it never closes a human gate.\n# Runbook-authoring allocation ledger\n\nThis readable companion to [`runbook-authoring-manifest.json`](runbook-authoring-manifest.json) is the persistent allocation ledger for the mock-fantasy-quest-4 authoring system. The JSON is authoritative for IDs, provenance, dependencies, output paths, counters, and recovery state. Accepted IDs are immutable: resume from the first non-accepted batch and append only at `B0007` and `R0013`.\n\n## Wave 1 \u2014 foundation contracts\n\n| Order | Batch | R contracts (maximum 7) | Dependency / parallel disposition | Checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | B0001 | R0001 | GATE-0001; no parallel work | Record gate and R0001 ownership |\n| 2 | B0002 | R0002, R0003, R0010 | After B0001 | Validate seams, fixtures, tokens and retained gates |\n| 3 | B0003 | R0004, R0008 | After B0002 | Validate state slice and P0 service exclusion |\n| 4 | B0004 | R0005, R0006 | After B0003; R0006 waits for GATE-0003 | Validate content/save separation |\n| 5 | B0005 | R0007, R0009, R0011 | After B0004; R0007/GATE-0003 and R0009/GATE-0005 remain blocked | Validate migration, diagnostics, provenance |\n| 6 | B0006 | R0012 | After B0003 and B0004; safe in parallel with B0005 after B0004 | Cross-check foundation documentation and gates |\n\nThe batches are deliberately smaller than seven where a contract is context-heavy (state, persistence, content) or tightly coupled to its predecessor. Together they form an incremental foundation vertical slice: reproducible baseline, shared seams/test harness, deterministic game state and P0 exclusion, content and recovery boundaries, then provenance/diagnostic controls and documented closure. They do not imply that the game is implemented.\n\n## Contract allocation and asset assessment\n\n| R ID | IMP source | Writer | Output | Asset assessment |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| R0001 | IMP-0001 | B0001 | `runbooks/R0001.md` | not applicable \u2014 tooling boundary only |\n| R0002 | IMP-0002 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0002.md` | not applicable \u2014 interfaces only |\n| R0003 | IMP-0003 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0003.md` | not applicable \u2014 deterministic fixtures only |\n| R0004 | IMP-0004 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0004.md` | not applicable \u2014 state envelopes only |\n| R0005 | IMP-0005 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0005.md` | not applicable \u2014 schema reserves later asset fields |\n| R0006 | IMP-0006 | B0004 | `runbooks/R0006.md` | not applicable \u2014 save/recovery protocol |\n| R0007 | IMP-0007 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0007.md` | not applicable \u2014 migrations only |\n| R0008 | IMP-0008 | B0003 | `runbooks/R0008.md` | not applicable \u2014 negative service scope |\n| R0009 | IMP-0009 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0009.md` | not applicable \u2014 local diagnostics only |\n| R0010 | IMP-0010 | B0002 | `runbooks/R0010.md` | not applicable \u2014 no final art/fonts/icons |\n| R0011 | IMP-0011 | B0005 | `runbooks/R0011.md` | not applicable \u2014 provenance control only |\n| R0012 | IMP-0012 | B0006 | `runbooks/R0012.md` | not applicable \u2014 documentation only |\n\nNo Wave 1 contract creates a product asset. Each contract still carries an explicit `asset_impact`, empty `asset_ids`, and an asset brief in the JSON; its author must prohibit incidental asset creation. A later asset-required contract must reserve stable product-specific IDs and a brief before assignment.\n\n## Author packet and resume rules\n\nEach B writer receives only the baseline terminology, target constraints, templates, authoring rules, and the named specification/catalogue records in its JSON `context_packet`. Every R record has canonical `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, `authoring_batch`, and `factory_stages` provenance. Use [`runbooks/TEMPLATE.md`](../../../runbooks/TEMPLATE.md) plus the F014 metadata and asset requirements.\n\nAt every checkpoint, validate unique B/R IDs, output-path ownership, dependency closure, provenance, the batch maximum, asset assessment, and retained human gates. On failure, retain all assigned IDs, repair the bounded failed output, and resume that same batch. Never regenerate accepted contracts or renumber the ledger.\n\n## Queued expansion chapters\n\n| Chapter | Future allocation point | State |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| SHARD-02 \u2014 Campaign content and domain logic | Wave 2 after GATE-0004 | queued |\n| SHARD-03 \u2014 Session, UI, input, and accessibility | Wave 3 after SHARD-02 and GATE-0002/0003/0004 | queued |\n| SHARD-04 \u2014 PWA delivery, performance, operations, release evidence | Wave 4 after SHARD-02/03 and GATE-0002/0005/0007 | queued |\n| SHARD-05 \u2014 Connected-scope decision package | Only explicit authorisation after GATE-0006; no P0 R allocation | hold |\n\nExpand one queued chapter into catalogue records before allocating its R IDs. This preserves the catalogue\u2019s dependency graph and makes a future thousand-runbook programme resumable without changing accepted work.\n\n## Structural check\n\n```sh\nnode -e 'const m=require(\"./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json\"); const r=m.r_contracts, b=m.batches, ids=a=>new Set(a).size===a.length; if (!ids(r.map(x=>x.id)) || !ids(b.map(x=>x.id)) || r.length!==m.expected_runbook_count || b.some(x=>x.r_contract_ids.length>7) || r.some(x=>!x.source_specifications.length || !x.source_catalogue_ids.length || !x.authoring_batch || !x.factory_stages.length || ![\"required\",\"not_applicable\"].includes(x.asset_impact) || !x.output_path || (x.asset_impact===\"required\" && (!x.asset_ids.length || !x.asset_brief))) ) process.exit(1); console.log(\"runbook authoring ledger structural check passed\")'\n```\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' runbooks/TEMPLATE.md; rg -n -C 8 '## Release definition|## Architecture and requirement traceability|bootstrap|deterministic|lint|format|shared core|adapter' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md; git status --short; test -e projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0001.md && sed -n '1,80p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0001.md || true\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n---\ntask_id: T001\nsequence: 1\ntitle: Describe one small, reviewable change\nbrowser_impact: none\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nDescribe the smallest complete outcome. State what is in scope and, equally\nimportant, what must not be changed.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- The intended behaviour is implemented and documented where it affects users or developers.\n- Focused automated tests cover the change and pass.\n- No unrelated refactors, network calls, or credentials are introduced.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-1-# Platform appendix \u2014 web and PWA\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-2-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-3-Both selected targets share the same campaign, save semantics, original-content boundary, accessibility intent, and P0 requirements. No target may change campaign rules or make connection/account use mandatory.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-4-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-5-| Area | Responsive public web application | Progressive web app | Parity / policy |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-6-| --- | --- | --- | --- |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md:7:| Input | keyboard, mouse, touch, feature-detected gamepad | same adapters | REQ-0009; unavailable hardware has visible fallback |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-8-| Layout/accessibility | phone/tablet/desktop semantic responsive UI | same | REQ-0010/NFR-0004; exact matrix remains GATE-0002 |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-9-| Capability | browser launch; initial assets required before offline play | install affordance when browser permits | declined/unavailable installation never blocks web play |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-10-| Offline/data | local saves and preferences; no network required for campaign | cached assets enable offline run; saves are distinct from cache | NFR-0001\u20130003; storage choice GATE-0003 |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-11-| Update | normal browser reload may obtain a release | cache refresh/update activates only title/pause after confirmed save | known-good cache retained; rollout GATE-0005 |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-12-| Performance | measure load, bundle, startup, interaction and slow network | additionally measure warm/offline startup and cache size | numeric budgets/matrix not selected (GATE-0002) |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-13-| Distribution/policy | public web host not selected | manifest/service worker/host policy not selected | no store/certification claim; GATE-0005/0007 |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-14-| Privacy/errors | minimal consent-gated diagnostics and player recovery | same, including cache/storage recovery | NFR-0006, REQ-0012 |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md-15-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/platform-appendix.md:16:There are no selected native, TV, console, account, multiplayer, commerce, or live-service targets. Any later target receives its own adaptation row and requirements; it does not reinterpret the shared core.\n--\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-1-# 01 \u2014 Product brief\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-2-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-3-**Canonical normalized brief:** [`../PROJECT_BRIEF.md`](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md).\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-4-`INITIAL.md` remains the source of truth. This specification intentionally adds\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-5-no requirements or decisions beyond that document.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-6-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md:7:## Release definition\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-8-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-9-Deliver an original, small, offline-capable 2D single-player fantasy RPG for a\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-10-responsive public web application and installable PWA. The complete path is one\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-11-town, one dungeon, onboarding, readable narrative/quests, tutorialized\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-12-party-based turn-based combat, inventory/progression, save/load/recovery,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-13-settings/accessibility, and a short ending.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-14-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md-15-## Requirement handling\n--\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-1-# 03 \u2014 Technical, data, trust, and quality contract\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-2-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-3-**Inputs:** [`INITIAL.md`](../INITIAL.md), [product brief](01-product-brief.md),\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-4-and [feature model](02-feature-model.md). This contract makes the P0 offline\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-5-single-player release implementable without selecting a vendor or creating a\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-6-dependency on the contradictory connected-service requests. It is a design\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-7-contract, not permission to collect data or operate services.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-8-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:9:## Architecture and requirement traceability\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-10-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-11-The production architecture is a static, client-side 2D web game: a shared\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:12:deterministic domain core, browser presentation/input adapters, versioned\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-13-authored content, and local durable storage. The web host may deliver a signed\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-14-release bundle and service worker, but it is not a campaign authority. The\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-15-core must have no browser APIs, timers, network calls, or renderer dependencies.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-16-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-17-| Component / boundary | Requirement served | Contract |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-18-| --- | --- | --- |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-19-| Game shell and responsive renderer | J1, J3, J6; readable mobile-to-desktop play | Owns navigation, focus/visibility pause, semantic UI, visual settings, and rendering only. It requests domain commands and renders returned state/events. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:20:| Input adapters | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad accessibility | Convert platform events into a shared, remappable `InputAction` vocabulary; unavailable hardware is non-fatal. No adapter may mutate campaign state directly. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-21-| Deterministic rules core | Tutorial combat, quests, inventory, progression | Owns legal state transitions, seeded/randomness policy, combat resolution, reward ledger, and invariants. Command validation precedes every mutation. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-22-| Content registry and validator | One town/dungeon, original content, localisation | Loads the active versioned content package; resolves IDs/keys and rejects invalid packages before play. It is read-only at runtime. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-23-| Save/recovery repository | J5, long-session recovery | Owns atomic local snapshot writes, validation, quarantine, migration, retention, export/import when approved, and explicit deletion. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-24-| PWA release manager | J7, install/offline/update safety | Owns precache manifest, cache integrity, install affordance, and safe-boundary activation. It never replaces a running release or a pending save write. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-25-| Error boundary and consent-gated diagnostics | Crash/error recovery and privacy | Shows retry/title/recovery actions. Local diagnostic records are minimal and removable; no telemetry transport exists in P0. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-26-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-27-### Dependency direction and state rules\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-28-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-29-```text\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:30:authored content \u2500\u2500> content validator \u2500\u2500> deterministic rules core <\u2500\u2500 input actions\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-31-                                            \u2502\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-32-web/PWA shell + renderer <\u2500\u2500 state/events \u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500> save/recovery repository\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-33-        \u2502                                   \u2502\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-34-        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500> PWA release manager \u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500> error boundary / local diagnostics\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-35-```\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-36-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-37-Only the rules core can produce durable campaign state. The shell owns\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-38-ephemeral UI state (open panels, selection, animation), while the repository\n--\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-67-  itemDefinitions[], rewards[], localisation[], assetManifest[] }\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-68-SaveSnapshot { schemaVersion, contentPackageId, contentVersion, slotId,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-69-  revision, checkpointId, campaign, quests, party, inventory, rewardLedger,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-70-  settingsReference?, checksum, committedAt }\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-71-```\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-72-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-73-- **Ownership and authoring:** designated game/content owners maintain source\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-74-  data in repository-controlled files and original asset references. The exact\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:75:  editor/format is an implementation choice; generated bundles are derived,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-76-  never hand-edited.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-77-- **Validation:** CI validates JSON/schema shape, unique stable IDs, required\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-78-  fields, asset and localisation-key existence, valid quest predicates,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-79-  scene/spawn/exit targets, legal reward/item/enemy references, no duplicate\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-80-  reward IDs, and reachability from opening scene to ending. It also rejects\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-81-  missing required translations in any release locale and unsafe quest-item\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-82-  removal paths.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-83-- **Relationship rules:** every required quest path has a reachable transition;\n--\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-111-- Use stable `rewardId`/`grantId` ledger entries and interaction IDs. Retrying\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-112-  an acknowledged reward, quest completion, or checkpoint returns the prior\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-113-  result rather than granting twice.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-114-- One command is processed at a time per active campaign. Disable/debounce\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-115-  duplicate controls while pending; compare expected revision on completion.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-116-  Visibility changes, gamepad repeats, and multiple tabs cannot interleave\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-117-  transitions. A second tab must be warned/read-only or acquire an explicit\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-118-  local slot lease; stale writers fail without replacing the latest revision.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:119:- Combat resolution uses an explicit deterministic seed/state recorded at\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-120-  safe boundaries. It never persists an unresolved turn; a crash restores the\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-121-  prior checkpoint, avoiding half-consumed items, half-applied damage, or\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-122-  duplicate rewards.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-123-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-124-## Persistence, migration, and failure semantics\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-125-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-126-Save commit is a write-ahead replacement protocol: serialize a complete\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-127-snapshot \u2192 validate checksum/schema/invariants \u2192 write candidate under a new\n--\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-135-| Storage quota, permission, or unavailable local storage | Keep session playable; show non-blocking save failure and retry guidance; do not claim persistence succeeded. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-136-| Interrupted/crashed write | Ignore incomplete candidate, retain/reload prior confirmed snapshot, and show recovery state at next launch. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-137-| Malformed/checksum-invalid save | Quarantine bytes and metadata; do not overwrite; offer last valid checkpoint/new game and support/export guidance where feasible. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-138-| Migration failure or unsupported version | Preserve original, fail closed into quarantine, and offer clear version/fallback choices. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-139-| Bad content package/reference | Fail build/CI; if discovered at runtime, stop the affected route, retain save, and offer title/retry rather than corrupting campaign state. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-140-| Cache/network refresh failure | Continue with known-good cached release; explain first-install offline limitation without pretending an uncached release is playable. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-141-| Application fault/crash loop | Error boundary offers retry, title, and safe recovery; diagnostics remain local unless explicitly consented and a future transport is approved. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-142-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:143:Migration functions are ordered, pure transformations from one explicit schema\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-144-version to the next, with fixtures for every supported predecessor. They must\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-145-be idempotent or record the source version so a retry cannot apply a migration\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-146-twice. A release may remove old migrations only after a separately approved\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-147-support/retention decision.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-148-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-149-## Trust, privacy, and security\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-150-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-151-- Treat content, imported saves, URL parameters, browser events, service-worker\n--\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-170-- No copied branding, assets, text, layouts, or distinctive interaction\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-171-  sequences enter the content pipeline. Provenance/review is required for\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-172-  shipped art, audio, writing, and dependencies.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-173-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-174-## Target-platform matrix\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-175-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-176-| Contract area | Responsive public web application | Installable PWA |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-177-| --- | --- | --- |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:178:| Shared code/data boundary | Same rules core, content package, save schema, semantic actions, accessibility settings, and release compatibility rules. Browser adapter owns viewport/input differences. | Same shared core/data. Service worker, installation, cache lifecycle, and platform install affordance are PWA adapters. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-179-| Input and device capability | Keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad where exposed; feature-detect and show available mappings. Handle resize, rotation, focus/visibility, reduced-motion/contrast settings where available. | Same inputs and feature detection; no assumed hardware merely because installed. Installation, notifications, or storage-persistence prompts are optional and must not block play. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-180-| Offline and recovery | May play offline only after relevant assets are cached. First uncached visit tells player an initial download/network is required. Local save protocol applies. | Primary offline route after successful install/cache. Precaches approved shell/content, retains known-good cache on refresh failure, and keeps saves independent of cache eviction. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-181-| Build and test | Produce hashed static assets, manifest, and browser-compatible bundle. Test core/unit/content validation, responsive layouts, input adaptations, save corruption/migration, error states, and supported browser/device/network matrix. | Build includes service worker and install manifest. Test installability, offline reload, cache rollback/update-at-safe-boundary, storage pressure, and all shared checks. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-182-| Accessibility and performance | Keyboard-operable menus, semantic labels, visible focus, readable scalable text, captions, non-colour cues, settings persistence, responsive phone/tablet/desktop checks. Establish approval-gated performance budgets for initial load, interaction frame time, memory, bundle/cache size, and slow-network behaviour before release. | Same accessibility/performance requirements, plus offline messaging, update state, install prompt alternatives, and no inaccessible full-screen/install-only path. Measure cold/warm/offline startup and cache size. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-183-| Release channel and policy | Immutable versioned static release to approved web hosting; staged/canary and rollback process must be selected before launch. Publish supported-browser matrix, privacy notice if diagnostics changes, and recovery notes. | Browser-mediated install/update channel; manifest/icon/display policy and service-worker behaviour must meet supported browser/platform rules. Prompt only at safe boundaries; release notes and rollback retain prior runnable cache. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-184-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-185-Support targets are expressed as capability tests rather than invented minimum\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-186-OS versions: current and previous major releases of agreed evergreen browsers\n--\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-193-P0 observability is local and player-respecting: structured console/build logs\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-194-in development, release/version/cache status visible in support UI, and an\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-195-optional bounded local fault record. It has no remote monitoring dependency.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-196-Every fault has a player-facing recovery path and a test that asserts retained\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-197-save/cache behaviour as applicable.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-198-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-199-| Evidence layer | Required checks |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-200-| --- | --- |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:201:| Rules and data | Unit/property tests for legal/illegal commands, revision rejection, idempotent grants, deterministic combat fixtures, save encode/decode/checksum, migration fixtures, recovery/quarantine, and content schema/reachability/reference validation. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-202-| Integration | Test shell-to-core action mapping, checkpoint ordering, focus/pause, unavailable input fallback, settings persistence failure, cache/save independence, and PWA safe update behaviour. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-203-| Target/browser | Run agreed browser/device matrix for keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad-adapted paths, responsive viewport checks, offline first/repeat visit, install flow where supported, storage failure, and browser accessibility audit/manual assistive-technology checks. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-204-| Performance/security | Capture approved load/frame/memory/cache budgets on representative low-capability and slow-network profiles; dependency/license/provenance review, CSP/header check, no-secret scan, and import/parser fuzz/bounds tests. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-205-| Release gate | Versioned content/save compatibility declaration, clean content validation, migration/recovery suite, PWA offline/update/rollback check, accessibility and performance results, privacy/diagnostic confirmation, and tested rollback to a known-good release. |\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-206-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-207-Release is blocked when validation cannot prove campaign reachability, a save\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-208-could overwrite the last known-good state, an update can activate mid-combat or\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-209-mid-write, an unsupported capability blocks the core path without explanation,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-210-or any mandatory quality budget/matrix remains unapproved or unmeasured.\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-211-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-212-## Decisions deferred for approval\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-213-\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-214-These are intentionally not selected by this contract: game engine/framework,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-215-rendering library, static host/CDN, CI provider, diagnostics vendor/transport,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-216-exact storage API/quota, browser/OS/device minimums, performance thresholds,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-217-languages, age/parental/regional obligations, service-worker rollout policy,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:218:and content editor format. Account identity, remote save replication,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-219-multiplayer/player safety, live operations, analytics, purchases, and\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-220-entitlements require separate product, security, privacy, cost, failure-mode,\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md-221-and support approval before they are allowed to alter this architecture.\n M README.md\n m supervisor\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest-2/\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest-3/\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/\n?? projects/mock-fantasy-quest/\n?? runbooks/.supervisor-children/\n\ncodex\n{\"status\":\"pass\",\"summary\":\"The allocated contract is intentionally gate-blocked. I will capture that containment in R0001: it specifies the post-decision bootstrap work but selects no framework, package manager, CI, or product implementation.\",\"changed_files\":[],\"test_result\":\"\",\"evidence\":{\"agent_log\":\"\",\"adapter_log\":\"\",\"test_log\":\"\",\"browser_log\":\"\",\"screenshots\":[]},\"acceptance_results\":[],\"documentation\":{\"reviewed_files\":[],\"updated_files\":[],\"summary\":\"\"},\"known_limitations\":[],\"browser_coverage\":\"\",\"recommended_next_step\":\"complete\"}\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -C 5 'F014|asset_impact|source_catalogue_ids|authoring_batch|factory_stages|provenance' . -g '*.md' -g '*.json' | head -260\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n./README.md-98-| Series | Meaning | Created by | Where it lives | What it does |\n./README.md-99-| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n./README.md-100-| `INITIAL.md` | Source brief | You or `supervisor initial` | `projects/<project-name>/` | The concise source brief: what is being made, for whom, where it will run, constraints, references, and desired outcome. It gives every later stage its context. |\n./README.md-101-| `F001`\u2013`F016` | **Factory** | This repository | `runbooks/` | **Factory runbooks.** They analyse the brief, write the detailed product plan, and create the files that will write the final implementation instructions. They do not build the requested product. |\n./README.md-102-| `B0001`, `B0002`, \u2026 | **Bounded authoring batch** | The F-series and later B dispatcher files | `projects/<slug>/authoring-runbooks/` | **Runbook-writing tasks.** A B task writes the next small set of R files. \u201cBounded\u201d means it has a hard limit: one B task may write no more than seven R files. |\n./README.md:103:| `R0001`, `R0002`, \u2026 | **Real product-work runbook** | B-series runbook-writing tasks | `projects/<slug>/runbooks/` | **Product-work runbooks.** Each R file gives detailed instructions and success checks for one small piece of building, testing, reviewing, documenting, or original-asset work. Every R file explicitly declares asset metadata and provenance links to its canonical specification, catalogue records, B batch, and originating F stages. |\n./README.md-104-\n./README.md-105-In short:\n./README.md-106-\n./README.md-107-```text\n./README.md-108-your brief \u2192 F-series factory \u2192 B-series runbook writers \u2192 R-series product work\n--\n./README.md-127-| --- | --- | --- |\n./README.md-128-| `F001` | `projects/<name>/INITIAL.md` | The user\u2019s original brief is the source of truth. F001 normalizes it but never replaces it. |\n./README.md-129-| `F002`\u2013`F004` | `INITIAL.md`, `PROJECT_BRIEF.md`, and the relevant earlier specification chapters | These stages refine product/domain, technical, and experience decisions. They use the documents produced by earlier F stages\u2014not every earlier F Markdown procedure. |\n./README.md-130-| `F005`\u2013`F010` | The canonical `specification/` chapters and earlier planning outputs | These stages translate established decisions into dependencies, delivery areas, authoring contracts, and a safe handoff. |\n./README.md-131-| `F011`\u2013`F013` | The canonical specification plus the delivery/contract planning outputs | These stages create the traceability system, implementation catalogue, dependency graph, and small B-series batches. |\n./README.md:132:| `F014`\u2013`F016` | The catalogue, authoring manifest, quality gate, and canonical specification | These stages create B writers, validate their intended R contracts, and register the child collections that Supervisor follows. |\n./README.md-133-| A B-series writer | Its own F013 context packet: only the assigned catalogue records, relevant specification sections, target constraints, templates, and reserved IDs | It writes no more than seven R files without needing a huge prompt or unrelated product context. |\n./README.md:134:| An R-series task | Its own objective, dependencies, acceptance criteria, and provenance metadata | It performs one small piece of product work. It can open the linked canonical files when it needs more detail. |\n./README.md-135-\n./README.md:136:Each generated R file must declare this provenance in its front matter:\n./README.md-137-\n./README.md-138-```yaml\n./README.md-139-source_specifications: specification/03-technical-contract.md#save-state\n./README.md:140:source_catalogue_ids: IMP-SAVE-001\n./README.md:141:authoring_batch: B0007\n./README.md:142:factory_stages: F003,F005,F012,F013\n./README.md-143-```\n./README.md-144-\n./README.md-145-These fields answer different questions:\n./README.md-146-\n./README.md-147-- `source_specifications` \u2014 which canonical requirement sections explain the work.\n./README.md:148:- `source_catalogue_ids` \u2014 which planned implementation records the task covers.\n./README.md:149:- `authoring_batch` \u2014 which bounded B writer created this R task.\n./README.md:150:- `factory_stages` \u2014 which F stages established the underlying requirement.\n./README.md-151-\n./README.md:152:The F and B references provide **provenance**, not a reason to use procedural\n./README.md-153-factory Markdown as implementation requirements. The canonical specification\n./README.md-154-and planning files remain the authoritative context. R-to-R dependencies define\n./README.md-155-execution order; Supervisor also follows explicit `.supervisor-children/`\n./README.md-156-registrations from the F collection to the B collection and then to the R\n./README.md-157-collection.\n./README.md-158-\n./README.md-159-After every B-series authoring wave, run that project's structural quality gate\n./README.md-160-(for example, `python3 projects/<project-name>/planning/validate_runbook_generation.py`).\n./README.md:161:It checks authoring provenance, ownership, assets, browser-test reservations,\n./README.md-162-and coverage; it is not evidence of product implementation, testing, or release\n./README.md-163-readiness.\n./README.md-164-\n./README.md-165-### Terms used in this repository\n./README.md-166-\n--\n./README.md-195-| `F009` | Audits the emerging catalogue against the brief, dependencies, targets, quality constraints, lifecycle, and omitted-but-necessary work. | A gap-checked catalogue with omissions, conflicts, and decisions recorded. |\n./README.md-196-| `F010` | Resolves the specification-to-authoring handoff: stable scope boundaries, task granularity, acceptance evidence, and the inputs needed to write implementation runbooks. | Final authoring inputs; no product code is created. |\n./README.md-197-| `F011` | Builds the canonical specification and requirements-traceability system, so every planned area can be traced back to the brief, a constraint, or a justified dependency. | Canonical specification and traceability records. |\n./README.md-198-| `F012` | Builds the full implementation catalogue and its dependency graph, including foundations, features, content, assets, quality, operations, and release work as applicable. | The complete checklist from which R-series tasks will be written. |\n./README.md-199-| `F013` | Divides that checklist into small, dependency-safe authoring batches that can scale from a handful to thousands of R files. | Batch plan and B-series dispatch strategy. |\n./README.md:200:| `F014` | Creates and registers the first B-series runbook writers. **It does not create R files directly.** Each B file writes at most seven detailed R-series product-work runbooks; later dispatcher B files create more B files when needed. | `authoring-runbooks/B\u2026.md` files. |\n./README.md:201:| `F015` | Checks that the B batches and their intended R contracts cover the catalogue, obey dependency ordering, and include `asset_impact` plus stable `asset_ids` wherever an R task needs assets. | Validated authoring batches and implementation-contract rules. |\n./README.md-202-| `F016` | Publishes the project-scoped handoff: registers generated collections, records how Supervisor should continue, and leaves the workspace ready for durable resume. | Handoff documentation and registered B/R collections. |\n./README.md-203-\n./README.md-204-The division of responsibility is therefore:\n./README.md-205-\n./README.md-206-- `F001`\u2013`F004`: understand and specify the particular product.\n./README.md-207-- `F005`\u2013`F010`: make its delivery work coherent, complete, and authorable.\n./README.md-208-- `F011`\u2013`F013`: turn that into a traceable catalogue and scalable batch plan.\n./README.md:209:- `F014`\u2013`F016`: create, validate, and hand off the B-series writers that produce R-series product work.\n./README.md-210-\n./README.md-211-## The product brief drives the result\n./README.md-212-\n./README.md-213-The factory treats `INITIAL.md` as the source of truth. It does not assume that\n./README.md-214-every product is a game or a web application.\n--\n./projects/README.md-39-only that size limit. It does not build the application. An R-series file is a\n./projects/README.md-40-*product-work instruction*: it contains the detailed steps and success evidence\n./projects/README.md-41-for one small piece of real product work.\n./projects/README.md-42-\n./projects/README.md-43-In other words, the step that creates the R files is not an F-series file:\n./projects/README.md:44:`F014` creates the first B files, then running each B file creates its R files.\n./projects/README.md-45-\n./projects/README.md-46-For a very large product, a B dispatcher file creates more B-series runbook\n./projects/README.md-47-writers for the remaining areas of the checklist. This is how the collection\n./projects/README.md-48-scales to hundreds or thousands of R-series files without oversized prompts or\n./projects/README.md-49-a separate manual run. The parent collection explicitly registers its generated\n--\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md-42-The `Final Fantasy V` reference is a high-level party-based, turn-based\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md-43-adventure scope reference only. Never copy or request its branding, names,\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md-44-story/lore, dialogue, maps, art, music, layouts, combat screens, or distinctive\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md-45-interaction choreography. All creative work must be independently original and\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md-46-follow the warm hand-painted, readable-silhouette, restrained-palette direction\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md:47:with provenance and human creative review.\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md-48-\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md-49-## Run the factory and authoring collections\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md-50-\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md-51-Run these from the repository root. `--project mock-fantasy-quest-4` loads this\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/README.md-52-workspace's versioned, non-secret Supervisor configuration and uses its durable\n--\n./runbooks/README.md-19-| F-series | The factory: understand the brief, identify the required domains, and design a complete delivery plan. | A workspace in `projects/<slug>/`, including its specification, work checklist, and B-series files. |\n./runbooks/README.md-20-| B-series | The runbook writers: write the next small set of implementation instructions. \u201cBounded\u201d means one B file may write no more than seven R files. | Up to seven R-series files per B task; extra B files are created when more areas need coverage. |\n./runbooks/README.md-21-| R-series | The product-work instructions: describe one small, ordered piece of real work. | Source changes, tests, assets, documentation, or other deliverables required by that R file. |\n./runbooks/README.md-22-\n./runbooks/README.md-23-Every R-series file must make an explicit asset decision in its front matter:\n./runbooks/README.md:24:`asset_impact: required` with stable comma-separated `asset_ids`, or\n./runbooks/README.md:25:`asset_impact: not_applicable` with a blank `asset_ids`. Asset-required files\n./runbooks/README.md-26-also describe asset purpose, variants, ownership, original-art constraints, and\n./runbooks/README.md-27-verification in an `## Asset assessment` section. This lets the Supervisor use\n./runbooks/README.md-28-its opt-in asset lane only where the product work genuinely needs it.\n./runbooks/README.md-29-\n./runbooks/README.md-30-Every R-series file also makes an explicit audio decision: `audio_impact:\n./runbooks/README.md-31-required` with stable `audio_ids`, a cue brief, duration, loop policy, and\n./runbooks/README.md-32-audio-style version; or `audio_impact: not_applicable` with the documented\n./runbooks/README.md-33-empty/default values. When required, Supervisor uses the independent local\n./runbooks/README.md-34-audio lane\u2014cue director, ACE-Step 1.5 XL Turbo generator, then technical and\n./runbooks/README.md:35:provenance QA\u2014before the coding stage. F004 creates an audio-direction and cue\n./runbooks/README.md-36-map only when the product actually needs music, sound effects, voice, or\n./runbooks/README.md-37-audible feedback.\n./runbooks/README.md-38-\n./runbooks/README.md:39:Every R-series file must also carry provenance metadata: `source_specifications`\n./runbooks/README.md-40-links to the smallest relevant canonical specification sections,\n./runbooks/README.md:41:`source_catalogue_ids` identifies its implementation-catalogue records,\n./runbooks/README.md:42:`authoring_batch` names its B-series writer, and `factory_stages` records the\n./runbooks/README.md-43-F-series stages that established the requirement. This lets an implementation\n./runbooks/README.md-44-agent retrieve focused canonical context without treating procedural F/B files\n./runbooks/README.md-45-as its requirements source.\n./runbooks/README.md-46-\n./runbooks/README.md-47-For example, a game may eventually have R runbooks for story, combat, content,\n--\n./runbooks/README.md-49-editing, debugging, and extensions. The F-series derives those areas from the\n./runbooks/README.md-50-brief rather than assuming either example.\n./runbooks/README.md-51-\n./runbooks/README.md-52-`F001`\u2013`F004` understand the brief and domains, `F005`\u2013`F010` make the delivery\n./runbooks/README.md-53-map, `F011`\u2013`F013` create and check the specification and work checklist, and\n./runbooks/README.md:54:`F014` creates the first B-series runbook-writing files. When Supervisor runs\n./runbooks/README.md-55-those B files, each one writes up to seven R-series product-work runbooks. If\n./runbooks/README.md-56-more R files are required, B dispatcher files create more B files. `F015`\u2013`F016`\n./runbooks/README.md-57-establish quality and handoff material.\n./runbooks/README.md-58-\n./runbooks/README.md-59-## Run the collection\n--\n./supervisor/README.md-553-is mutually exclusive with `--retry` and `--verify`.\n./supervisor/README.md-554-\n./supervisor/README.md-555-### Optional local asset lane\n./supervisor/README.md-556-\n./supervisor/README.md-557-Runbooks can explicitly opt into a local original-art lane without changing\n./supervisor/README.md:558:the normal code pipeline. A task with `asset_impact: required` runs an\n./supervisor/README.md-559-art-director brief, local ComfyUI generator, deterministic asset finisher and\n./supervisor/README.md:560:provenance/technical asset QA before the first coding agent. It then continues\n./supervisor/README.md-561-through the usual Codex verification, deterministic tests, Playwright, visual\n./supervisor/README.md-562-evidence, completion audit and Git publisher.\n./supervisor/README.md-563-\n./supervisor/README.md-564-```yaml\n./supervisor/README.md:565:asset_impact: required\n./supervisor/README.md-566-asset_brief: docs/art/briefs/my-asset.md\n./supervisor/README.md-567-asset_ids: village_gate_001,village_gate_001_construction\n./supervisor/README.md-568-visual_style_version: project-v1\n./supervisor/README.md-569-```\n./supervisor/README.md-570-\n--\n./runbooks/F013.md-28-  expansion chapter. Every allocated `R-*` ID is unique and immutable.\n./runbooks/F013.md-29-- Batch ordering respects the catalogue dependency graph and identifies batches\n./runbooks/F013.md-30-  safe to author in parallel versus those requiring a preceding decision.\n./runbooks/F013.md-31-- Each batch has a context packet listing only the relevant specification,\n./runbooks/F013.md-32-  catalogue records, terminology, target constraints, required templates, and\n./runbooks/F013.md:33:  explicit provenance values for every allocated R contract: canonical\n./runbooks/F013.md:34:  `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, originating\n./runbooks/F013.md:35:  `authoring_batch`, and the relevant `factory_stages`.\n./runbooks/F013.md-36-- No authoring batch is assigned more than seven R-series contracts; a smaller\n./runbooks/F013.md-37-  batch is required when its contracts are tightly coupled or context-heavy.\n./runbooks/F013.md-38-- The manifest records expected runbook count, IDs, dependencies, output paths,\n./runbooks/F013.md-39-  acceptance checks, checkpoint cadence, recovery instructions, and an explicit\n./runbooks/F013.md-40-  asset assessment for every allocated R contract. The assessment records\n./runbooks/F013.md:41:  `asset_impact` as either `required` or `not_applicable`; asset-required\n./runbooks/F013.md-42-  contracts reserve stable, product-specific `asset_ids` and identify their\n./runbooks/F013.md-43-  asset brief/purpose before a B-series writer is assigned.\n./runbooks/F013.md-44-- The manifest records an explicit audio assessment for every allocated R\n./runbooks/F013.md-45-  contract. It uses `audio_impact` (`required` or `not_applicable`); audio\n./runbooks/F013.md-46-  contracts reserve stable, product-specific `audio_ids`, a cue brief, duration,\n--\n./runbooks/F015.md-16-\n./runbooks/F015.md-17-## Acceptance criteria\n./runbooks/F015.md-18-\n./runbooks/F015.md-19-- Validation checks required front matter, unique IDs, sequence ordering,\n./runbooks/F015.md-20-  requirement links, dependency closure, output ownership, and unsupported\n./runbooks/F015.md:21:  circular dependencies. Every R contract must also have non-empty provenance:\n./runbooks/F015.md:22:  canonical `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, its originating\n./runbooks/F015.md:23:  `authoring_batch`, and valid `factory_stages`; the links must resolve to the\n./runbooks/F015.md-24-  manifest and canonical specification rather than merely naming an F or B\n./runbooks/F015.md-25-  procedure file.\n./runbooks/F015.md-26-- Validation checks every R-series contract has an explicit asset assessment:\n./runbooks/F015.md:27:  `asset_impact` is `required` or `not_applicable`, `asset_ids` is present,\n./runbooks/F015.md-28-  asset-required contracts reserve non-empty unique stable IDs and an asset\n./runbooks/F015.md-29-  brief/style version, and non-asset contracts do not silently introduce asset\n./runbooks/F015.md-30-  work. It reports missing, duplicate, ambiguous, or unowned asset IDs.\n./runbooks/F015.md-31-- Validation checks every R-series contract has an explicit audio assessment:\n./runbooks/F015.md-32-  `audio_impact` is `required` or `not_applicable`; audio-required contracts\n--\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md-9-| Narrative/quests/tutorial/ending | SPEC-02; Quest, Dialogue, Campaign; predicates/reward-once/ending | scenes, inventory, combat, save | original text/localisation keys and quest graph | P0; quantities/locales gated |\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md-10-| Combat/party/rewards | SPEC-02; Party, Encounter; legal turns/defeat/checkpoints | quests, inventory, save | encounters/actions/reward definitions | P0; party size/balance gated |\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md-11-| Inventory/progression | SPEC-02; Item, inventory; legal use/protected quest items | quests, party, save | item/equipment definitions | P0 |\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md-12-| Persistence/recovery | SPEC-02/03; snapshot/checkpoint; atomic/version/quarantine rules | all durable game state, PWA cache | schema/migrations/fixtures | P0, storage policy gated |\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md-13-| Accessibility/localisation/feedback | SPEC-04; preferences/options; semantic/reflow/non-colour rules | shell, input, content | strings, captions, tokens, feedback assets | P0; exact targets/locales/assets gated |\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md:14:| Content/original assets | SPEC-02/03; ContentPackage; validation/reachability/provenance | all gameplay and UI | source manifest, assets, locale, validator | P0; original boundary explicit |\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md-15-| PWA/release/quality | SPEC-03; cache/version/release; safe activation/rollback | persistence, shell, content | manifest/precache/release metadata/evidence | P0 PWA, operations thresholds gated |\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md-16-| Privacy/diagnostics | SPEC-03/04; diagnostic record; consent/redaction/deletion | recovery, release | diagnostic schema and disclosure | P0 local-minimal; transport/retention gated |\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md-17-| Account/sync | SPEC-00; potential identity/replication/conflict rules | save/privacy/security | none until approved | hold, GATE-0006 |\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md-18-| Multiplayer/safety/live service/commerce | SPEC-00; service/safety/entitlement models not authored | privacy/security/operations/legal | none until approved | hold, GATE-0006 |\n./projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/domain-completeness-review.md-19-\n--\n./runbooks/F014.md-1----\n./runbooks/F014.md:2:task_id: F014\n./runbooks/F014.md-3-sequence: 14\n./runbooks/F014.md-4-title: Generate the bounded implementation-runbook authoring collection\n./runbooks/F014.md-5-browser_impact: not_applicable\n./runbooks/F014.md-6-playwright_spec:\n./runbooks/F014.md-7----\n--\n./runbooks/F014.md-39-and contain: precise objective; dependencies; relevant requirement IDs; owned\n./runbooks/F014.md-40-files/data/interfaces; required behaviour; non-goals; platform differences;\n./runbooks/F014.md-41-verification commands/evidence; and observable acceptance criteria. It must be\n./runbooks/F014.md-42-small enough for one implementation task, not a vague epic.\n./runbooks/F014.md-43-\n./runbooks/F014.md:44:Every generated R-series runbook must retain machine-readable provenance to the\n./runbooks/F014.md-45-canonical decisions that justify it. Its front matter must declare all of these\n./runbooks/F014.md-46-non-empty fields, using comma-separated values where more than one applies:\n./runbooks/F014.md-47-\n./runbooks/F014.md-48-```yaml\n./runbooks/F014.md-49-source_specifications: specification/03-technical-contract.md#save-state\n./runbooks/F014.md:50:source_catalogue_ids: IMP-SAVE-001\n./runbooks/F014.md:51:authoring_batch: B0007\n./runbooks/F014.md:52:factory_stages: F003,F005,F012,F013\n./runbooks/F014.md-53-```\n./runbooks/F014.md-54-\n./runbooks/F014.md-55-`source_specifications` points to the smallest relevant canonical\n./runbooks/F014.md:56:specification section; `source_catalogue_ids` identifies the allocated\n./runbooks/F014.md:57:implementation-catalogue records; `authoring_batch` identifies the B-series\n./runbooks/F014.md:58:writer that created the R file; and `factory_stages` identifies the F-series\n./runbooks/F014.md-59-stages that established the requirement. This is a traceability trail, not an\n./runbooks/F014.md-60-instruction to inject every F or B runbook into the implementation agent's\n./runbooks/F014.md-61-prompt. The R task uses these links to read only the canonical context it needs.\n./runbooks/F014.md-62-\n./runbooks/F014.md-63-Every generated R-series runbook must also include a detailed asset assessment.\n./runbooks/F014.md-64-Its front matter must always declare both fields below, even when no assets are\n./runbooks/F014.md-65-needed:\n./runbooks/F014.md-66-\n./runbooks/F014.md-67-```yaml\n./runbooks/F014.md:68:asset_impact: required # or not_applicable\n./runbooks/F014.md-69-asset_ids: village_gate_main,village_gate_construction # blank only when not_applicable\n./runbooks/F014.md-70-```\n./runbooks/F014.md-71-\n./runbooks/F014.md:72:For `asset_impact: required`, the B-series writer must reserve stable,\n./runbooks/F014.md-73-product-specific asset IDs (never generic names such as `image1`), provide an\n./runbooks/F014.md-74-`asset_brief` and `visual_style_version`, and include an `## Asset assessment`\n./runbooks/F014.md-75-section explaining the assets' purpose, where they appear, the required visual\n./runbooks/F014.md-76-states/variants, source-of-truth ownership, and original-art acceptance checks.\n./runbooks/F014.md-77-For `not_applicable`, that section must explain why the scope needs no generated\n--\n./runbooks/F014.md-94-\n./runbooks/F014.md-95-For `audio_impact: required`, the writer must reserve stable cue IDs, cite the\n./runbooks/F014.md-96-relevant `04-audio-direction.md` section, describe the in-product trigger and\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '30,130p' runbooks/F014.md; sed -n '1,180p' runbooks/F015.md; sed -n '1,200p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/validate_runbook_generation.py 2>/dev/null || true; rg --files projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4 | rg 'validate.*runbook|R0001|runbooks'\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n\nEvery dispatcher first expands at most one queued specification/catalogue domain\nchapter (maximum 250 `IMP-*` contracts), validates its coverage, then allocates\nready contracts. If any queued chapter or ready contract remains, it creates no\nmore than ten authoring tasks and exactly one successor dispatcher. If none\nremains, it creates the final audit task instead. This is a durable loop, not a\nrecursive prompt or an unbounded single run.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must use the standard Supervisor front matter\nand contain: precise objective; dependencies; relevant requirement IDs; owned\nfiles/data/interfaces; required behaviour; non-goals; platform differences;\nverification commands/evidence; and observable acceptance criteria. It must be\nsmall enough for one implementation task, not a vague epic.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must retain machine-readable provenance to the\ncanonical decisions that justify it. Its front matter must declare all of these\nnon-empty fields, using comma-separated values where more than one applies:\n\n```yaml\nsource_specifications: specification/03-technical-contract.md#save-state\nsource_catalogue_ids: IMP-SAVE-001\nauthoring_batch: B0007\nfactory_stages: F003,F005,F012,F013\n```\n\n`source_specifications` points to the smallest relevant canonical\nspecification section; `source_catalogue_ids` identifies the allocated\nimplementation-catalogue records; `authoring_batch` identifies the B-series\nwriter that created the R file; and `factory_stages` identifies the F-series\nstages that established the requirement. This is a traceability trail, not an\ninstruction to inject every F or B runbook into the implementation agent's\nprompt. The R task uses these links to read only the canonical context it needs.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must also include a detailed asset assessment.\nIts front matter must always declare both fields below, even when no assets are\nneeded:\n\n```yaml\nasset_impact: required # or not_applicable\nasset_ids: village_gate_main,village_gate_construction # blank only when not_applicable\n```\n\nFor `asset_impact: required`, the B-series writer must reserve stable,\nproduct-specific asset IDs (never generic names such as `image1`), provide an\n`asset_brief` and `visual_style_version`, and include an `## Asset assessment`\nsection explaining the assets' purpose, where they appear, the required visual\nstates/variants, source-of-truth ownership, and original-art acceptance checks.\nFor `not_applicable`, that section must explain why the scope needs no generated\nor source visual asset and explicitly prohibit incidental asset creation. This\nkeeps UI, illustration, icon, texture, sprite, audio-visual, and other product\nasset work visible in the implementation plan instead of discovering it midway\nthrough coding.\n\nEvery generated R-series runbook must also declare an audio assessment, even\nwhen audio is not needed:\n\n```yaml\naudio_impact: required # or not_applicable\naudio_ids: ember_menu_loop # blank only when not_applicable\naudio_brief: 30-second seamless instrumental menu cue # blank only when not_applicable\naudio_duration_seconds: 30 # 0 only when not_applicable\naudio_loop: required # required, not_required, or not_applicable\naudio_style_version: project-audio-v1\n```\n\nFor `audio_impact: required`, the writer must reserve stable cue IDs, cite the\nrelevant `04-audio-direction.md` section, describe the in-product trigger and\nmix/accessibility expectations, and assign a sensible duration and loop policy.\nThe Supervisor's separate local audio lane then creates a manifest, invokes\nACE-Step, and performs technical/provenance QA before coding begins. For\n`not_applicable`, every audio field has the stated empty/default form so an\notherwise unrelated task cannot silently generate music or sound effects.\n\nEach B-series authoring task may create at most seven R-series implementation\nrunbooks. It must stop after its assigned seven (or fewer), update the manifest,\nand leave remaining work for the next bounded batch.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- The initial collection contains only a bounded bootstrap wave; each\n  dispatcher expands at most one 250-contract domain shard, creates at most ten\n  new B-series authoring tasks plus one successor, then exits. It never attempts\n  to materialise the whole game plan.\n- There is exactly one valid B-series authoring runbook per allocated manifest\n  batch, and it creates no more than seven assigned R-series runbooks.\n- B-series runbooks never overwrite an existing R-series contract with a\n  conflicting ID; they stop and report mismatches.\n- Every generated B-series runbook includes its context packet, output list,\n  validation procedure, checkpoint/update procedure, and a clear stop condition.\n- Both child-collection registration files exist, contain valid JSON with a\n  relative `runbooks_dir`, and point to the generated authoring and\n  implementation collections respectively.\n- R-series contracts use stable IDs and parse with the Supervisor runbook\n  loader; their declared dependencies match the manifest, every contract has\n  explicit `asset_impact` and `asset_ids` metadata, and asset-required\n  contracts have non-empty stable IDs. Every R contract also has non-empty,\n  valid `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, `authoring_batch`,\n  and `factory_stages` provenance metadata.\n- Every R-series contract has explicit valid audio metadata. Audio-required\n  contracts have stable IDs, a non-empty cue brief, duration, loop policy,\n  audio-style version, and a source link to the canonical audio direction.\n---\ntask_id: F015\nsequence: 15\ntitle: Validate generated authoring batches and implementation contracts\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nCreate `projects/<project-slug>/planning/runbook-generation-quality-gate.md`\nand validation instructions/scripts that can be run after every B-series wave.\nValidate the authoring collection itself and the R-series contracts it produces.\nThe validation is structural and traceability-based; it must not claim the\nproduct has been implemented or released.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Validation checks required front matter, unique IDs, sequence ordering,\n  requirement links, dependency closure, output ownership, and unsupported\n  circular dependencies. Every R contract must also have non-empty provenance:\n  canonical `source_specifications`, `source_catalogue_ids`, its originating\n  `authoring_batch`, and valid `factory_stages`; the links must resolve to the\n  manifest and canonical specification rather than merely naming an F or B\n  procedure file.\n- Validation checks every R-series contract has an explicit asset assessment:\n  `asset_impact` is `required` or `not_applicable`, `asset_ids` is present,\n  asset-required contracts reserve non-empty unique stable IDs and an asset\n  brief/style version, and non-asset contracts do not silently introduce asset\n  work. It reports missing, duplicate, ambiguous, or unowned asset IDs.\n- Validation checks every R-series contract has an explicit audio assessment:\n  `audio_impact` is `required` or `not_applicable`; audio-required contracts\n  reserve non-empty unique stable cue IDs, a brief, a positive duration, loop\n  policy, style version, and source link to `04-audio-direction.md`; unrelated\n  contracts do not silently introduce music or sound-effect work.\n- It detects duplicate browser test paths and confirms browser-impact metadata\n  only where browser behaviour is actually in scope.\n- It reports catalogue/manifest/specification coverage counts and identifies\n  every missing, duplicate, blocked, or unverified contract by ID.\n- It fails the quality gate when a discovered domain family has no specification\n  chapter, content/authoring approach where applicable, implementation\n  contracts, or traceable verification. It checks category-appropriate\n  completeness rather than applying a fixed checklist to every product.\n- It includes recovery: how to rerun one failed B-series authoring task, repair\n  a bounded R-series range, and resume the next dispatcher wave without\n  regenerating accepted work.\n- Human gates, paid services, credentials, legal/compliance decisions, device\n  access, and store/certification actions remain explicit review points.\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Structural quality gate for generated B-series and R-series runbooks.\n\nThis validates planning artefacts only; it is never product or release evidence.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nimport argparse\nimport json\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\nREPOSITORY = ROOT.parents[1]\nMANIFEST_PATH = ROOT / \"planning/runbook-authoring-manifest.json\"\nCATALOGUE_PATH = ROOT / \"planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json\"\nR_ID, F_ID = re.compile(r\"^R\\d{4}$\"), re.compile(r\"^F\\d{3}$\")\n\ndef values(value):\n    return value if isinstance(value, list) else ([value] if value else [])\n\ndef parse_value(value):\n    value = value.strip().strip(\"\\\"'\")\n    if value == \"[]\": return []\n    if value.startswith(\"[\") and value.endswith(\"]\"):\n        return [item.strip().strip(\"\\\"'\") for item in value[1:-1].split(\",\") if item.strip()]\n    return value\n\ndef front_matter(path):\n    text = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    if not text.startswith(\"---\\n\"): return {}\n    end = text.find(\"\\n---\", 4)\n    if end < 0: return {}\n    result, key = {}, None\n    for line in text[4:end].splitlines():\n        if line.lstrip().startswith(\"-\") and key:\n            result.setdefault(key, []).append(parse_value(line.split(\"-\", 1)[1]))\n        elif \":\" in line:\n            key, value = line.split(\":\", 1); key = key.strip(); result[key] = parse_value(value)\n    return result\n\ndef headings(path):\n    return {re.sub(r\"[^a-z0-9]+\", \"-\", match.group(1).lower()).strip(\"-\")\n            for match in re.finditer(r\"^#{1,6}\\s+(.+?)\\s*$\", path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"), re.M)}\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--wave\", help=\"validate allocations through this B batch\")\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n    manifest = json.loads(MANIFEST_PATH.read_text())\n    catalogue = json.loads(CATALOGUE_PATH.read_text())\n    batches = {b[\"id\"]: b for b in manifest[\"batches\"]}\n    contracts = manifest[\"r_contracts\"]\n    if args.wave:\n        if args.wave not in batches: parser.error(\"unknown B batch: \" + args.wave)\n        contracts = [c for c in contracts if batches.get(c.get(\"authoring_batch\"), {}).get(\"order\", 999) <= batches[args.wave][\"order\"]]\n    records = {r[\"id\"]: r for r in catalogue[\"contracts\"]}\n    errors, missing, blocked, unverified = [], [], [], []\n    def fail(text): errors.append(text)\n    ids = [c.get(\"id\", \"\") for c in contracts]\n    if ids != sorted(ids): fail(\"manifest R IDs are not sequence ordered\")\n    for item, count in Counter(ids).items():\n        if count > 1: fail(\"duplicate manifest R ID \" + item)\n    paths = [c.get(\"output_path\", \"\") for c in contracts]\n    for item, count in Counter(paths).items():\n        if count > 1: fail(\"duplicate output path \" + item)\n    assets, browser_paths = {}, defaultdict(list)\n    # The authoring collection is part of the gate: each allocated batch must\n    # exist, identify itself, and be the sole owner of its manifest R IDs.\n    for batch_id, batch_record in batches.items():\n        batch_path = ROOT / \"authoring-runbooks\" / f\"{batch_id}.md\"\n        meta = front_matter(batch_path) if batch_path.is_file() else {}\n        if not meta: fail(f\"{batch_id}: missing or invalid B-series front matter\")\n        elif meta.get(\"task_id\") != batch_id: fail(f\"{batch_id}: task ID mismatch in authoring runbook\")\n        if len(batch_record.get(\"r_contract_ids\", [])) > 7: fail(f\"{batch_id}: exceeds seven-contract batch limit\")\n    for contract in contracts:\n        rid, batch = contract.get(\"id\", \"\"), contract.get(\"authoring_batch\", \"\")\n        if not R_ID.fullmatch(rid): fail(f\"invalid R ID {rid!r}\")\n        if batch not in batches or rid not in batches.get(batch, {}).get(\"r_contract_ids\", []): fail(f\"{rid}: authoring batch ownership mismatch\")\n        catalogue_ids = values(contract.get(\"source_catalogue_ids\"))\n        if not catalogue_ids or contract.get(\"catalogue_id\") not in catalogue_ids: fail(f\"{rid}: missing or inconsistent catalogue provenance\")\n        for cid in catalogue_ids:\n            if cid not in records: fail(f\"{rid}: unknown catalogue ID {cid}\")\n        if contract.get(\"catalogue_id\") in records and not records[contract[\"catalogue_id\"]].get(\"requirement_ids\"): fail(f\"{rid}: catalogue record lacks requirement links\")\n        specs = values(contract.get(\"source_specifications\"))\n        if not specs: fail(f\"{rid}: missing source_specifications\")\n        for link in specs:\n            file_name, _, anchor = link.partition(\"#\"); source = ROOT / file_name\n            if not link.startswith(\"specification/\") or not source.is_file() or (anchor and anchor not in headings(source)):\n                fail(f\"{rid}: unresolved canonical specification link {link}\")\n        stages = values(contract.get(\"factory_stages\"))\n        if not stages or any(not F_ID.fullmatch(s) for s in stages): fail(f\"{rid}: invalid factory_stages\")\n        for dependency in values(contract.get(\"dependencies\")):\n            if dependency not in ids: fail(f\"{rid}: dependency {dependency} is outside allocation\")\n            elif dependency >= rid: fail(f\"{rid}: dependency {dependency} does not precede contract\")\n        impact, asset_ids = contract.get(\"asset_impact\"), values(contract.get(\"asset_ids\"))\n        if impact not in {\"required\", \"not_applicable\"}: fail(f\"{rid}: invalid asset_impact\")\n        if \"asset_ids\" not in contract: fail(f\"{rid}: asset_ids absent\")\n        if impact == \"required\":\n            if not asset_ids or not contract.get(\"asset_brief\") or not contract.get(\"visual_style_version\"): fail(f\"{rid}: required assets lack IDs, brief, or style version\")\n            for asset in asset_ids:\n                if not re.fullmatch(r\"ASSET-[A-Z0-9-]+\", asset): fail(f\"{rid}: unstable asset ID {asset}\")\n                if asset in assets: fail(f\"asset {asset} ambiguously owned by {assets[asset]} and {rid}\")\n                assets[asset] = rid\n        elif asset_ids: fail(f\"{rid}: non-asset contract reserves asset IDs\")\n        if batches.get(batch, {}).get(\"state\") in {\"blocked_on_gate\", \"blocked\", \"repairable\"}: blocked.append(rid)\n        r_path = REPOSITORY / contract.get(\"output_path\", \"\")\n        if not r_path.is_file(): missing.append(rid); continue\n        meta = front_matter(r_path)\n        for field in (\"task_id\", \"sequence\", \"source_specifications\", \"source_catalogue_ids\", \"authoring_batch\", \"factory_stages\", \"asset_impact\", \"asset_ids\"):\n            if field not in meta: fail(f\"{rid}: {r_path.name} lacks {field}\")\n        if \"requirement_ids\" not in meta: fail(f\"{rid}: {r_path.name} lacks requirement_ids\")\n        if meta.get(\"task_id\") != rid: fail(f\"{rid}: task ID mismatch\")\n        if values(meta.get(\"requirement_ids\")) != records.get(contract.get(\"catalogue_id\"), {}).get(\"requirement_ids\", []): fail(f\"{rid}: requirement_ids differ from catalogue\")\n        for field in (\"source_specifications\", \"source_catalogue_ids\", \"authoring_batch\", \"factory_stages\", \"asset_impact\", \"asset_ids\"):\n            if values(meta.get(field)) != values(contract.get(field)): fail(f\"{rid}: {field} differs from manifest\")\n        browser, specs = meta.get(\"browser_impact\", \"not_applicable\"), values(meta.get(\"playwright_spec\", meta.get(\"playwright_specs\")))\n        if browser == \"not_applicable\" and specs: fail(f\"{rid}: browser path on non-browser contract\")\n        if browser != \"not_applicable\" and (not specs or not set(records.get(contract.get(\"catalogue_id\"), {}).get(\"targets\", [])) & {\"web\", \"pwa\"}): fail(f\"{rid}: browser metadata outside browser behaviour scope\")\n        for spec in specs: browser_paths[spec].append(rid)\n        body = r_path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        if \"## Verification\" not in body: unverified.append(rid)\n        if not re.search(r\"^## Asset assessment\\s*$\", body, re.M | re.I): fail(f\"{rid}: missing explicit Asset assessment section\")\n    for path, owners in browser_paths.items():\n        if len(owners) > 1: fail(f\"duplicate browser test path {path}: {', '.join(owners)}\")\n    graph = {c[\"id\"]: values(c.get(\"dependencies\")) for c in contracts}; active, done = set(), set()\n    def visit(node):\n        if node in active: fail(\"unsupported circular dependency through \" + node); return\n        if node not in done:\n            active.add(node)\n            for dep in graph[node]:\n                if dep in graph: visit(dep)\n            active.remove(node); done.add(node)\n    for node in graph: visit(node)\n    # Discovered families need a canonical source and a category-appropriate\n    # catalogue/verification route. Explicitly held connected scope is a valid\n    # reviewed outcome, not a missing P0 implementation contract.\n    review = ROOT / \"specification/domain-completeness-review.md\"\n    for line in review.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines():\n        if not line.startswith(\"|\") or line.startswith(\"| System\") or line.startswith(\"| ---\"): continue\n        cells = [cell.strip() for cell in line.split(\"|\")[1:-1]]\n        if len(cells) != 5: continue\n        family, source, _deps, authoring, decision = cells\n        if \"SPEC-\" not in source: fail(f\"domain family {family}: no specification chapter\")\n        if \"hold\" in decision.lower(): continue\n        keywords = [word.lower() for word in re.findall(r\"[A-Za-z]{5,}\", family)]\n        searchable = json.dumps(catalogue).lower()\n        if not any(word in searchable for word in keywords): fail(f\"domain family {family}: no catalogue traceability\")\n        if authoring.lower() not in {\"none\", \"n/a\"} and not authoring: fail(f\"domain family {family}: no content/authoring approach\")\n    allocated = {c.get(\"catalogue_id\") for c in contracts}\n    unallocated = [cid for cid in records if cid not in allocated]\n    print(\"Runbook-generation structural quality gate (not product/release evidence)\")\n    chapter_count = len(list((ROOT / \"specification\").glob(\"*.md\")))\n    source_link_count = sum(len(values(c.get(\"source_specifications\"))) for c in contracts)\n    print(f\"canonical specification chapters: {chapter_count}\")\n    print(f\"specification provenance links: {source_link_count}\")\n    for label, result in ((\"catalogue contracts\", list(records)), (\"manifest contracts\", ids), (\"R files missing\", missing), (\"blocked contracts\", blocked), (\"unverified contracts\", unverified), (\"unallocated catalogue contracts\", unallocated), (\"reserved asset IDs\", list(assets)), (\"browser test paths\", list(browser_paths))):\n        print(f\"{label}: {len(result)}\" + (\" (\" + \", \".join(sorted(result)) + \")\" if result else \"\"))\n    for error in errors: print(\"ERROR:\", error)\n    return 1 if errors or missing or unverified else 0\nif __name__ == \"__main__\": sys.exit(main())\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/planning/validate_runbook_generation.py\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/B0003.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/B0002.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/B0006.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/C0001.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/D0001.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/B0001.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/B0005.md\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/authoring-runbooks/INITIAL.md\nprojects/mock-fantas